    gpiod_line_set_value(line, 1);

    // Wait for duration
    while (!exit_flag && duration > 0) {
        if (gpiod_line_get_value(line, &event) == -1) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Error getting GPIO line value: %s\n", gpiod_error());
            gpiod_line_release(line);
            gpiod_chip_close(line);
            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
        }
        if (event.value == 1) {
            duration--;
            printf("Valve on for %d seconds\n", duration);
        }
        usleep(100000);
    }

    // Deactivate valve
    printf("Valve off\n");
    gpiod_line_set_value(line, 0);

    // Release GPIO line
    gpiod_line_release(line);
    gpiod_chip_close(line);

    return 0;
}
```

This code uses the `gpiod` library to interact with the GPIO bus on the Raspberry Pi. It reads the configuration file, parses the duration, initializes the GPIO line, activates the valve, waits for the duration, and then deactivates the valve. The `sig_handler` function is installed to handle SIGINT and SIGTERM signals, which sets the `exit_flag` variable to 1. The main loop checks this flag and turns off the valve before exiting. On every exit path, the GPIO line is released and the chip is closed to ensure the valve is always left off.

